REVISION 1: Composite Pattern Structure Alignment
Problem / Question Considered:
    The UML diagram modeled WorkItemComponent as an independent base class, causing CompositeWorkItem to inherit from WorkItemComponent rather than
    AbstractWorkItem. Additionally, CompositeWorkItem stored a child list typed strictly to CompositeWorkItem.
Original Design / Assumption:
    CompositeWorkItem relied on a separate WorkItemComponent hierarchy, and its internal container could only hold other composite nodes.
Change Made:
    Removed WorkItemComponent. Made CompositeWorkItem inherit directly from AbstractWorkItem. Updated child list storage in CompositeWorkItem to
    List<AbstractWorkItem*>.
Why Change Was Made:
    To strictly comply with the GoF Composite Pattern. Inheriting from AbstractWorkItem allows CompositeWorkItem to act as a standard Component.
    Typing the child list to AbstractWorkItem* enables recursive composition of leaf nodes (WorkItem), sub-composites, and decorated items.
Updates to Relevant Diagrams:
    UML Class Diagram: Removed WorkItemComponent block. Rerouted CompositeWorkItem generalization arrow to AbstractWorkItem.
    

REVISION 2: Open/Closed Principle (OCP) in WorkItemState
Problem / Question Considered:
    Concrete states declared transition action methods (such as paused(), retry(), failedDelivery(), exception()) that were missing from the abstract base class WorkItemState.
Original Design / Assumption:
    WorkItemState declared only nextState(), forcing callers or WorkItem to know concrete state types or rely on non-polymorphic routing.
Change Made:
    Added virtual method signatures for all state transition actions (paused(), resume(), retry(), failed(), exception(), validate(), operation()) to the base
    WorkItemState class.
Why Change Was Made:
    To eliminate Open/Closed Principle (OCP) violations and prevent the need for type checks, dynamic casting, or switch/if-else chains when invoking
    state transitions polymorphically through a WorkItemState* pointer.
Updates to Relevant Diagrams:
    UML Class Diagram: Updated WorkItemState base class block to display all transition action virtual methods.
    

REVISION 3: Observer Responsibility Decoupling from Mediator
Problem / Question Considered:
    WorkflowMediator contained observer list management functionality (-observerList: Observer*, attach(), detach(), notify()).
Original Design / Assumption:
    The mediator acted as both a colleague coordinator and an observer subscriber registry.
Change Made:
    Removed observer list attributes and attach()/detach() methods from WorkflowMediator.
    AbstractWorkItem remains the sole Subject base class managing subscriber lists (-observers: List<Observer*>).
Why Change Was Made:
    To preserve single-responsibility separation between the Mediator pattern (multi-colleague orchestration) and the Observer pattern (state-change notifications).
Updates to Relevant Diagrams:
    UML Class Diagram: Removed -observerList, attach(), detach(), and notify() from WorkflowMediator.

REVISION 4: Removing the Strategy Design Pattern from TIVIDY
Problem / Question Considered: 
    Identifying algorithms applicable across various industries. 
Original Design / Assumption: 
    Assumed algorithm classes could simply output a function call trace without concrete functionality. 
Change Made: 
    Removed the Strategy design pattern from the architecture. 
Why Change Was Made: 
    The pattern could not provide generic functionality useful across diverse industry workflows. 
Updates to Relevant Diagrams: 
    UML Class Diagram: Removed the Strategy pattern.

REVISION 5: Scenarios become workflow definitions run by the generic engine
Problem / Question Considered:
    The exam and game scenarios were built as separate class hierarchies (ExamPaper, Marker, GameWorker, their own states, strategies and
    facades) beside TIVIDY. The scenario was defining its own architecture, which the brief forbids: "Your selected scenario must not
    determine the complete architecture of the generic workflow manager", and our research found "all process rules live in the
    definition, not the engine" (WfMC, 1996).
Original Design / Assumption:
    Each scenario needed its own classes and its own UI. The generic engine hard-coded its escalation chain
    (Team lead -> Manager -> Department head -> Director -> Executive) and its external system.
Change Made:
    The escalation chain (1 to 5 named levels with rising limits), the external system's name and the scoring rule are now set at run time
    through the facade (setEscalationLevels, setExternalSystem, setStrategy). Added scoreTask and named template copies so instances
    can be marked and told apart. The web UI loads a workflow definition (exam marking, game studio, software project, or one the user
    builds) that also names the work, its parts, its states and its extra behaviours. The scenario code and pages stay in the repository
    but are no longer built or served.
Why Change Was Made:
    So one engine runs any process without being rewritten, as the brief and our research require. Exam marking is now Tutor ->
    Assistant lecturer -> Lecturer -> Module coordinator escalation, a master paper template copied per student (Prototype),
    questions as scored tasks (Strategy), concessions as Decorators and a semester test as a Composite, all on the generic classes.
Updates to Relevant Diagrams:
    Generic Class Diagram: add setEscalationLevels(), getEscalationLevels(), setExternalSystem(), getExternalSystem(), scoreTask() and
    createFromTemplate(index, name) to TIVIDY and the matching operations to WorkflowMediator; add -levels to WorkflowMediator; add
    -name, setName() and getName() to Adaptee; add rename() to AbstractWorkItem. The scenario class diagrams become object/configuration
    examples of the generic diagram rather than separate designs.
